import{_ as a,o as r,c as t,a2 as i}from"./chunks/framework.Bx6eOp9J.js";const h=JSON.parse('{"title":"Agent Research & Competitor Intelligence · 2026-11-09","description":"Catch-up since November 5: async supervisors, durable tasks, enterprise authority, delivery evidence and competitor governance.","frontmatter":{"title":"Agent Research & Competitor Intelligence · 2026-11-09","description":"Catch-up since November 5: async supervisors, durable tasks, enterprise authority, delivery evidence and competitor governance.","outline":[2,3]},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/runtime/daily-intelligence/2026-11-09.md","filePath":"en/runtime/daily-intelligence/2026-11-09.md","lastUpdated":1794188794000}'),s={name:"en/runtime/daily-intelligence/2026-11-09.md"};function n(o,e,l,c,d,p){return r(),t("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[i('<h1 id="agent-research-competitor-intelligence-·-2026-11-09" tabindex="-1">Agent Research &amp; Competitor Intelligence · 2026-11-09 <a class="header-anchor" href="#agent-research-competitor-intelligence-·-2026-11-09" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Agent Research &amp; Competitor Intelligence · 2026-11-09&quot;">​</a></h1><p>The central finding is a shift from parallel Agent calls toward responsive supervisors, durable background tasks, traceable authority and evidence-backed completion. Deep Agents&#39; async reference deserves priority experience; Paperclip&#39;s native Runner advances delivery; Paradev&#39;s cloud-to-host access and Palmier&#39;s auto-approval direction need separate enterprise-governance assessment.</p><p>This report is archived under the actual execution date, <strong>2026-11-09 (Asia/Shanghai)</strong>, catching up since the last successful monitor without fabricating November 6–8 daily reports. Execution began at 09:21:57 Shanghai (01:21:57 UTC); most complete commit-query baselines were November 5 at 05:43:53 UTC, with per-source local successful records authoritative. Event dates are UTC from original sources unless stated otherwise. Orkas #193 was verified during execution after the initial cutoff and is labelled separately. OPEN→MERGED and main→release are delivery/status advances, not newly invented mechanisms.</p><p>There are <strong>32 observations and 5 analyses</strong>. This is source collection and classification: no local product experiments, articles, formal development reviews or actual comments were completed.</p><h2 id="layer-1-factual-observations" tabindex="-1">Layer 1: factual observations <a class="header-anchor" href="#layer-1-factual-observations" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Layer 1: factual observations&quot;">​</a></h2><h3 id="o1-yue-zhao-s-audit-catalogue-merges-four-external-contributions" tabindex="-1">O1 Yue Zhao&#39;s audit catalogue merges four external contributions <a class="header-anchor" href="#o1-yue-zhao-s-audit-catalogue-merges-four-external-contributions" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O1 Yue Zhao&#39;s audit catalogue merges four external contributions&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>MERGED · 2026-11-05 UTC</strong></p><p>Yue Zhao&#39;s awesome-auditable-ai merged #47–50: Agent Role Contracts checks declared roles, task scopes, review separation and handoffs; Ponder uses an append-only JSONL ledger for review coverage/convergence; Tracekit moves decisions and signing into a separate process; SealedRun joins decision-record tools. Acceptance of contributed entries does not establish Zhao&#39;s authorship or verification of every claim. ARC does not grant runtime authority; Tracekit observes only mediated calls and its same-user development signer lacks strong isolation. SealedRun 0.8.0 was released November 1; the new event is catalogue inclusion. Classify as reference material and source-review candidates, not installed tools. Discussion: where do declaration checks, runtime authority and offline evidence verification each stop?</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/yzhao062/awesome-auditable-ai/pull/47" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a> · <a href="https://github.com/yzhao062/awesome-auditable-ai/pull/48" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">2</a> · <a href="https://github.com/yzhao062/awesome-auditable-ai/pull/49" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">3</a> · <a href="https://github.com/yzhao062/awesome-auditable-ai/pull/50" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">4</a></p><h3 id="o2-agents-sdk-application-owned-persistence-and-bounded-strict-executors" tabindex="-1">O2 Agents SDK: application-owned persistence and bounded strict executors <a class="header-anchor" href="#o2-agents-sdk-application-owned-persistence-and-bounded-strict-executors" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O2 Agents SDK: application-owned persistence and bounded strict executors&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>PRERELEASE · 2026-11-08 UTC</strong></p><p>Python v0.24.0-alpha.7 and JS v0.22.0-alpha.2 ship preview packages with native application-owned SessionStore and explicit unknown-outcome reconciliation. Initial persistence covers root function tools, not every child agent, MCP/manual tool or portable checkpoint; Python&#39;s SQLite reference targets single-host local POSIX. New environments default to strict with compatible executors; Windows/remote legacy requires explicit selection, and restoring old profiles does not upgrade their policy. This advances November 5 main-branch observations into prereleases. Learning: publish recovery/platform limits explicitly. Candidate: crash recovery with uncertain side effects; not executed.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/openai/openai-agents-python/releases/tag/v0.24.0-alpha.7" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a> · <a href="https://github.com/openai/openai-agents-js/releases/tag/v0.22.0-alpha.2" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">2</a></p><h3 id="o3-agents-sdk-budgets-supervision-and-a2a-retain-application-duties" tabindex="-1">O3 Agents SDK: budgets, supervision and A2A retain application duties <a class="header-anchor" href="#o3-agents-sdk-budgets-supervision-and-a2a-retain-application-duties" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O3 Agents SDK: budgets, supervision and A2A retain application duties&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>PRERELEASE · 2026-11-08 UTC</strong></p><p>The same native previews include RunBudget, before_complete review, context transforms, an A2A 1.0 polling adapter and cancellation cleanup. Budgets gate subsequent root request admission rather than imposing a hard spend ceiling; admitted requests may exceed the budget and child coverage is limited. Rejected completion text may already have streamed and remain in history, so review is not a pre-disclosure barrier. Applications still own current A2A authorization, durable storage and unknown mutation outcomes; cancellation does not undo external effects. Classify as protocol-adapter/Runtime governance experiment candidates, not automatic FCoP kernel requirements. Discussion: correlate admission, approval and uncertain outcomes in auditable events.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/openai/openai-agents-python/releases/tag/v0.24.0-alpha.7" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a> · <a href="https://github.com/openai/openai-agents-js/releases/tag/v0.22.0-alpha.2" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">2</a></p><h3 id="o4-codex-0-167-ships-session-continuity-and-approval-lifecycle-changes" tabindex="-1">O4 Codex 0.167 ships session continuity and approval lifecycle changes <a class="header-anchor" href="#o4-codex-0-167-ships-session-continuity-and-approval-lifecycle-changes" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O4 Codex 0.167 ships session continuity and approval lifecycle changes&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>RELEASED · 2026-11-06 UTC</strong></p><p>Stable rust-v0.167.0 adds a codex agents --no-daemon command centre and unread state. Chats remain readable across provider changes; when resumption is unavailable an editable prompt can start a new chat. Network approvals close with their request and are serialized per endpoint; compaction accounting includes failed responses and missing usage. The changelog also lists fresh human confirmation for managed prompt-user and turn-scoped tools. These distinguish visibility, resumability and restart preparation; they do not prove repair of this automation&#39;s remote routing failure. Classify as continued observation and recovery experience candidates.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/openai/codex/releases/tag/rust-v0.167.0" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a></p><h3 id="o5-codex-main-separates-project-write-access-from-project-trust" tabindex="-1">O5 Codex main separates project write access from project trust <a class="header-anchor" href="#o5-codex-main-separates-project-write-access-from-project-trust" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O5 Codex main separates project write access from project trust&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>MERGED · 2026-11-07 UTC</strong></p><p>#60041 requires explicit project trust in non-interactive execution: workspace-write is not a proxy for trust, untrusted project configuration/MCP is disabled, explicit trust persists even in ephemeral mode, and explicit distrust is not casually overridden. #60046 derives MCP network policy and preserves valid denials: denials are published before persistence, while grants wait for successful storage. Both merged after 0.167 was released; inclusion in that stable release is not claimed. Classify as enterprise authorization research. Discussion: failure ordering among trust configuration, persistence and immediate revocation.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/openai/codex/pull/60041" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a> · <a href="https://github.com/openai/codex/pull/60046" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">2</a></p><h3 id="o6-claude-code-makes-unattended-refusal-visible-in-exit-results" tabindex="-1">O6 Claude Code makes unattended refusal visible in exit results <a class="header-anchor" href="#o6-claude-code-makes-unattended-refusal-visible-in-exit-results" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O6 Claude Code makes unattended refusal visible in exit results&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>RELEASED · 2026-11-06 UTC</strong></p><p>In v2.1.319, auto mode asks or denies calls it cannot check instead of silently falling back to a local classifier, subject to provider/configuration exceptions. permissions.denyInsteadOfPrompting lets unattended workflows deny and continue. claude -p returns nonzero when refusal prevents completion; resumption preserves missing deferred MCP calls and refuses subsequent input. Background MCP tasks return before notification; cloud /loop lifetime remains container-bound. Learning: define automatic execution, continuation, failure and background lifetime separately. Classify as continued observation and refusal-propagation testing candidates.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code/releases/tag/v2.1.319" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a></p><h3 id="o7-adk-2-14-clarifies-environment-skill-execution-and-evaluation-interfaces" tabindex="-1">O7 ADK 2.14 clarifies environment, skill execution and evaluation interfaces <a class="header-anchor" href="#o7-adk-2-14-clarifies-environment-skill-execution-and-evaluation-interfaces" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O7 ADK 2.14 clarifies environment, skill execution and evaluation interfaces&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>RELEASED · 2026-11-07 UTC</strong></p><p>v2.14.0 was actually published November 7 UTC although its note heading says November 4. It stabilizes environment/A2A interfaces, adds chunked file reading and validated skill-script execution, and introduces MultiRunConfig, parallel evaluation repeats and configurable ToolArgsMatcher. Passing Enum members to tools is a compatibility change. Upstream reports 23% faster and 9% less memory on a 30-node/200-turn case; this was not reproduced locally or generalized. Classify interfaces/evaluation as references and skill execution/argument migration as experiment candidates.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/google/adk-python/releases/tag/v2.14.0" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a></p><h3 id="o8-adk-main-fixes-approval-arguments-resumption-and-shared-tool-lifetime" tabindex="-1">O8 ADK main fixes approval arguments, resumption and shared-tool lifetime <a class="header-anchor" href="#o8-adk-main-fixes-approval-arguments-resumption-and-shared-tool-lifetime" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O8 ADK main fixes approval arguments, resumption and shared-tool lifetime&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>MAIN COMMITS · 2026-11-06–07 UTC</strong></p><p>669f55b repairs confirmation predicates affected by Enum conversion: ordinary predicates retain raw enum values while tool functions still receive members, with a separate validated-argument path. ba142cf avoids rerunning already handled input when resuming wait_for_output unless rerun_on_resume explicitly requests it. d0f227a reference-counts shared toolsets so only the last holder closes them. These main commits were inspected; their individual inclusion in 2.14 was not established. Classify as Runtime recovery/authorization experiment candidates, without inferring matching CodeFlowMu defects.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/google/adk-python/commit/669f55bbaa75f49e59a2273c2ecbb534749c85b0" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a> · <a href="https://github.com/google/adk-python/commit/ba142cf8e91b045aa0b435f71dbd7c3e686efa45" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">2</a> · <a href="https://github.com/google/adk-python/commit/d0f227afc3c7187de875dda2e11ff4b47fe285cd" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">3</a></p><h3 id="o9-adk-evaluation-excludes-thought-text-from-delivered-answers" tabindex="-1">O9 ADK evaluation excludes thought text from delivered answers <a class="header-anchor" href="#o9-adk-evaluation-excludes-thought-text-from-delivered-answers" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O9 ADK evaluation excludes thought text from delivered answers&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>MAIN COMMIT · 2026-11-07 UTC</strong></p><p>dd7161f removes model thoughts from relevant evaluation input so judges assess answers/visible text; it does not uniformly rewrite Vertex&#39;s structured multi-turn path. The useful lesson is to fix the evaluated object: internal reasoning, tool traces and delivered artifacts are different. Main commit verified; release inclusion and local score impact remain unverified. Classify as evaluation-method experiments. Discussion: retain trajectory analysis and delivery grading as separately labelled measurements.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/google/adk-python/commit/dd7161f7a2b88cd7ae482cc2acd8423675ac027c" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a></p><h3 id="o10-langgraph-exposes-strict-resume-through-the-python-sdk" tabindex="-1">O10 LangGraph exposes strict_resume through the Python SDK <a class="header-anchor" href="#o10-langgraph-exposes-strict-resume-through-the-python-sdk" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O10 LangGraph exposes strict_resume through the Python SDK&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>MERGED + RELEASED · 2026-11-05–06 UTC</strong></p><p>#9599 ships in sdk==0.4.14, exposing strict_resume on synchronous/asynchronous create, stream and wait for thread runs. Stateless calls omit the field and older servers may ignore it. A strict client request therefore does not establish server-side enforcement without version/capability checks. This advances the November 5 private-field observation into public SDK delivery. Classify as recovery-contract experience candidates; map first to Runtime/adapters rather than automatically changing FCoP&#39;s kernel.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/langchain-ai/langgraph/pull/9599" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a> · <a href="https://github.com/langchain-ai/langgraph/releases/tag/sdk%3D%3D0.4.14" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">2</a></p><h3 id="o11-mcp-repairs-oneof-example-type-validation-boundaries" tabindex="-1">O11 MCP repairs oneOf example type-validation boundaries <a class="header-anchor" href="#o11-mcp-repairs-oneof-example-type-validation-boundaries" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O11 MCP repairs oneOf example type-validation boundaries&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>MERGED · 2026-11-06 UTC</strong></p><p>#3185 fixes documentation/schema examples where branch-local properties in oneOf could bypass intended type checks. Common properties move to the root and required sets express exclusive branches. This is an example correction, not a universal security patch across MCP SDKs or a new wire capability. Classify as protocol/schema reference material and counterexample-test candidates: state/message contracts need negative cases.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/modelcontextprotocol/pull/3185" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a></p><h3 id="o12-a2ui-and-ag2-demonstrate-wire-differences-behind-similar-version-labels" tabindex="-1">O12 A2UI and AG2 demonstrate wire differences behind similar version labels <a class="header-anchor" href="#o12-a2ui-and-ag2-demonstrate-wire-differences-behind-similar-version-labels" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O12 A2UI and AG2 demonstrate wire differences behind similar version labels&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>MERGED · 2026-11-06 / 08 UTC</strong></p><p>A2UI #3999 marks 1.0 current/stable and deprecates 0.8. AG2 #3618 follows the stable wire: RC v1.0 becomes 1.0, with TextField.value, capability-key and MIME corrections. AG2 still defaults to 0.9; known A2A capability-scoping limits remain, and a new AG2 release containing these changes was not verified. Learning: test version negotiation, catalogues and capabilities together. This is UI/adapter interoperability research, not evidence of a same-layer FCoP competitor.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/a2ui-project/a2ui/pull/3999" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a> · <a href="https://github.com/ag2ai/ag2/pull/3618" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">2</a></p><h3 id="o13-dspy-distinguishes-reusable-history-from-interpreter-state-restoration" tabindex="-1">O13 DSPy distinguishes reusable history from interpreter-state restoration <a class="header-anchor" href="#o13-dspy-distinguishes-reusable-history-from-interpreter-state-restoration" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O13 DSPy distinguishes reusable history from interpreter-state restoration&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>MERGED · 2026-11-08 UTC</strong></p><p>#10827 adds reusable dspy.History conversations to RLM. Each invocation still creates a fresh interpreter; earlier code is replayed as text rather than executed and Python variables are not restored. Current trajectory is separate from a compactable history copy. LocalInterpreter trusts its host and is not a substitute for a Deno/Pyodide security sandbox. Main merged; stable delivery unverified. Classify as memory/recovery research, comparing conversational continuity with actual execution-state continuity.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/stanfordnlp/dspy/pull/10827" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a></p><h3 id="o14-crewai-exposes-script-and-evaluation-failures-explicitly" tabindex="-1">O14 CrewAI exposes script and evaluation failures explicitly <a class="header-anchor" href="#o14-crewai-exposes-script-and-evaluation-failures-explicitly" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O14 CrewAI exposes script and evaluation failures explicitly&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>RELEASED · 2026-11-08 UTC</strong></p><p>1.15.38 returns nonzero on project-script failure, records evaluation errors as run_failed, adds per-evaluation usage/target spans and restricts .env tracing permissions. 1.15.39 exists later, but its documentation increment is not counted as another governance capability. Learning: external schedulers need failure-versus-completion signals rather than process termination alone; comprehensive failure semantics are not established. Classify as failure-propagation/observability references, without local reproduction.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/crewAIInc/crewAI/releases/tag/1.15.38" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a></p><h3 id="o15-paperclip-releases-native-runner-with-platform-and-migration-limits" tabindex="-1">O15 Paperclip releases native Runner with platform and migration limits <a class="header-anchor" href="#o15-paperclip-releases-native-runner-with-platform-and-migration-limits" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O15 Paperclip releases native Runner with platform and migration limits&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>RELEASED · 2026-11-06 UTC</strong></p><p>v2026.1106.0 defaults qualified new Claude/Codex/OpenCode/Pi agents to native Runner on supported macOS/Linux; Linux requires glibc&gt;=2.39. Existing agents retain settings and can opt into upgrade; Windows default support is not established. The release removes experimental Pipelines surfaces/implementation while retaining tables and Cases/Routines. Sentry run-failure reporting becomes explicit opt-in. Its 515 inter-release commits are not all new events in this run. Competitor learning/risk: high/high; raise native-delivery maturity with platform/migration limits. Experience candidate: new/existing employee upgrades.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/paperclipai/paperclip/releases/tag/v2026.1106.0" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a></p><h3 id="o16-paperclip-makes-skill-versions-telemetry-and-restore-points-operational-choices" tabindex="-1">O16 Paperclip makes skill versions, telemetry and restore points operational choices <a class="header-anchor" href="#o16-paperclip-makes-skill-versions-telemetry-and-restore-points-operational-choices" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O16 Paperclip makes skill versions, telemetry and restore points operational choices&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>RELEASED · 2026-11-06 UTC</strong></p><p>The same stable release adds repository-derived skill sources, roughly five-minute refresh, per-run version pinning and disconnection choices. Its model-based injection classifier requires configuration/opt-in and is not a default security guarantee. Telemetry is off by default, with OTLP/company credentials available. Workspace compaction/expiry uses encrypted restore points; an expired point can lead to a fresh workspace with an explanation, not exact restoration. Competitor learning/risk: high/high; maintain close watch. Study whether skill versions, evidence and recovery semantics can be traced together. Classify as operational-mechanism experience candidates.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/paperclipai/paperclip/releases/tag/v2026.1106.0" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a></p><h3 id="o17-paperclip-tightens-task-ownership-and-completion-races" tabindex="-1">O17 Paperclip tightens task ownership and completion races <a class="header-anchor" href="#o17-paperclip-tightens-task-ownership-and-completion-races" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O17 Paperclip tightens task ownership and completion races&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>MERGED · 2026-11-05 / 08 UTC</strong></p><p>#19186 advances from OPEN to MERGED: a reassigned-away run cannot close the new owner&#39;s task, while its result remains in history. #20108 waits up to 30 seconds for earlier held tool calls before judging finish/block, rejecting settlement when calls remain pending or results unseen. The latter postdates the November 6 release; the former&#39;s individual release inclusion was not checked. Competitor learning/risk: high/high; maintain close watch. Study who judges completion against ownership and unresolved evidence; this is not proof of global exactly-once execution.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/paperclipai/paperclip/pull/19186" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a> · <a href="https://github.com/paperclipai/paperclip/pull/20108" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">2</a></p><h3 id="o18-paradev-preserves-resumed-permissions-and-opens-an-explicit-cloud-to-host-boundary" tabindex="-1">O18 Paradev preserves resumed permissions and opens an explicit cloud-to-host boundary <a class="header-anchor" href="#o18-paradev-preserves-resumed-permissions-and-opens-an-explicit-cloud-to-host-boundary" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O18 Paradev preserves resumed permissions and opens an explicit cloud-to-host boundary&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>RELEASED + MERGED · 2026-11-06–08 UTC</strong></p><p>Verified Superset successor Paradev desktop-v2.0.6 includes permission-mode preservation across resume/fork/recovery (#9354) and owner-opened host access for owned cloud workspaces (#9408/#9409). The toggle is off by default and owner-only; requests recheck API authorization, boxes cannot enable it themselves, and WebSockets are rejected. Once enabled, owner-box code can drive the host as owner; the PR has no second host-side lock, so owner-only is not code safety. Removing Beta labels does not verify maturity. Competitor learning high, cross-host risk high; raise trust-boundary scrutiny. Candidates: revocation, injection and resumed permissions.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/ParadevAI/Paradev/releases/tag/desktop-v2.0.6" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a> · <a href="https://github.com/ParadevAI/Paradev/pull/9408" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">2</a> · <a href="https://github.com/ParadevAI/Paradev/pull/9354" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">3</a></p><h3 id="o19-palmier-improves-continuity-while-explicitly-adopting-auto-approval" tabindex="-1">O19 Palmier improves continuity while explicitly adopting auto-approval <a class="header-anchor" href="#o19-palmier-improves-continuity-while-explicitly-adopting-auto-approval" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O19 Palmier improves continuity while explicitly adopting auto-approval&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>RELEASED / DEFAULT-BRANCH COMMITS · 2026-11-06–09 UTC</strong></p><p>Commits around v1.1.26–28 force YOLO/auto-approval and remove the per-task approval path: a governance direction change, not stronger enterprise safety. need-input can end a CLI turn while retaining the Palmier run/browser and resume the same session; adapters unable to resume context are removed, and host standing prompts enter launches including scheduled tasks. NATS reconnect cleanup avoids duplicate RPC and a pending-run marker claims the exact run rather than guessing the newest. Competitor continuity learning is high, enterprise approval fit decreases, and risk scrutiny rises. These mechanisms still need independent validation and do not prove end-to-end duplicate-effect prevention.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/caihongxu/palmier/releases/tag/v1.1.28" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a> · <a href="https://github.com/caihongxu/palmier/commit/2e3c584fc97dcab37f0a20d17441f998cdeb9c8a" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">2</a> · <a href="https://github.com/caihongxu/palmier/commit/fe83ec62a2e97822e503b58eebe35695805fe71f" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">3</a> · <a href="https://github.com/caihongxu/palmier/commit/cf6b69d3308f116e38a949ce08dfbe97fe23e556" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">4</a></p><h3 id="o20-orca-strengthens-mobile-offline-control-and-pairing-identity" tabindex="-1">O20 Orca strengthens mobile offline control and pairing identity <a class="header-anchor" href="#o20-orca-strengthens-mobile-offline-control-and-pairing-identity" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O20 Orca strengthens mobile offline control and pairing identity&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>RELEASED · 2026-11-08 UTC</strong></p><p>v1.4.254 adds ordered mobile offline queues and preserves drafts, attachments, file edits and automation drafts across app termination. One-use pairing codes require the device to sign into its own identity; legacy-code compatibility remains marked. orca serve restricts public addresses by default with LAN/Tailscale exceptions, and new-repository trust differs from grandfathered repositories. Release notes do not establish exactly-once offline command execution. Competitor learning/risk: high/high; maintain close watch. Candidates: weak-network approval/pause/resume and identity revocation. Commit coverage stops at 400 records and remains incomplete.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/stablyai/orca/releases/tag/v1.4.254" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a></p><h3 id="o21-agentlas-update-cleanup-preserves-active-versions-and-rollback-room" tabindex="-1">O21 Agentlas update cleanup preserves active versions and rollback room <a class="header-anchor" href="#o21-agentlas-update-cleanup-preserves-active-versions-and-rollback-room" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O21 Agentlas update cleanup preserves active versions and rollback room&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>RELEASED · 2026-11-06–08 UTC</strong></p><p>os-v1.2.87 cleanup retains current, host-configured, running/recently used runtimes, rollback candidates and newer versions after manual rollback. Unknown usage prevents deletion, at most five versions are removed per run, and cleanup failure does not fail the update. .88 excludes failed translation batches from daily quota with bounded retries; .89 improves browser error handling. This is not comprehensive disaster recovery. Competitor learning high/risk medium-high; keep operational delivery under observation. Candidates: updating and rolling back with active workers and disk pressure, not version-count-based maturity.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/agentlas-ai/Agentlas-OS/releases/tag/os-v1.2.87" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a> · <a href="https://github.com/agentlas-ai/Agentlas-OS/releases/tag/os-v1.2.88" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">2</a> · <a href="https://github.com/agentlas-ai/Agentlas-OS/releases/tag/os-v1.2.89" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">3</a></p><h3 id="o22-openhands-ships-service-identity-and-explicit-credentials-for-automation" tabindex="-1">O22 OpenHands ships service identity and explicit credentials for automation <a class="header-anchor" href="#o22-openhands-ships-service-identity-and-explicit-credentials-for-automation" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O22 OpenHands ships service identity and explicit credentials for automation&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>RELEASED · 2026-11-05–06 UTC</strong></p><p>Canvas v1.36.0 lists service-account automations (#19033) and enterprise Git template/provider setup. SDK 1.60.0–1.60.2 includes explicitly approved secrets for Canvas backends, encryption of bash event files at rest and a large remote-conversation-state fix. Ordinary interface changes in v1.37.0 are not counted as another governance breakthrough. Service identity opens research into responsibility/credential lifecycle; tenant scopes, rotation and revocation still need checking. Feature titles do not establish full enterprise isolation. Classify as identity/recovery experience candidates. As a competitor, learning high/risk medium-high; maintain assessment.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/OpenHands/OpenHands/releases/tag/v1.36.0" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a> · <a href="https://github.com/OpenHands/software-agent-sdk/releases/tag/v1.60.0" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">2</a> · <a href="https://github.com/OpenHands/software-agent-sdk/releases/tag/v1.60.2" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">3</a> · <a href="https://github.com/OpenHands/OpenHands/pull/19033" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">4</a></p><h3 id="o23-orkas-isolates-unreadable-skills-and-preserves-final-execution-records" tabindex="-1">O23 Orkas isolates unreadable skills and preserves final execution records <a class="header-anchor" href="#o23-orkas-isolates-unreadable-skills-and-preserves-final-execution-records" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O23 Orkas isolates unreadable skills and preserves final execution records&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>MERGED · 2026-11-08–09 UTC</strong></p><p>#190 skips only an unreadable Skill, supports retry after repair and avoids persisting an incomplete catalogue as trusted state; root-directory failure stays explicit. #193 flushes a final Codex JSON-RPC record lacking a newline before stdout closes, retaining failure/interruption/provenance. It merged about one minute after this run&#39;s start and is separately labelled an event discovered during catch-up, not inserted into the earlier cutoff. Both are main changes without verified new stable delivery; Windows GUI/paid models were not tested. Competitor learning medium-high/risk medium; maintain radar. Study partial degradation without losing terminal evidence.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/Orkas-AI/Orkas/pull/190" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a> · <a href="https://github.com/Orkas-AI/Orkas/pull/193" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">2</a></p><h3 id="o24-langfuse-moves-read-export-auditing-into-stable-delivery" tabindex="-1">O24 Langfuse moves read/export auditing into stable delivery <a class="header-anchor" href="#o24-langfuse-moves-read-export-auditing-into-stable-delivery" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O24 Langfuse moves read/export auditing into stable delivery&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>RELEASED · 2026-11-05 UTC</strong></p><p>v4.76.0 delivers previously observed read-access auditing and prompt-cap rollback alongside export-download auditing and self-hosted gateway compose. Existing EE/opt-in and route-coverage limits remain; universal read auditing is not established. The new value is stable delivery and download evidence, not reinvention of earlier main changes. Classify as enterprise-audit references. Discussion: correlate reading, export, downloading and post-revocation access in one evidence chain.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/langfuse/langfuse/releases/tag/v4.76.0" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a></p><h3 id="o25-github-mcp-has-open-reports-on-read-only-switches-and-temporary-urls" tabindex="-1">O25 GitHub MCP has open reports on read-only switches and temporary URLs <a class="header-anchor" href="#o25-github-mcp-has-open-reports-on-read-only-switches-and-temporary-urls" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O25 GitHub MCP has open reports on read-only switches and temporary URLs&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>OPEN ISSUES · 2026-11-08 UTC</strong></p><p>#3747 reports that v2.1.0 parses GITHUB_READ_ONLY=yes/on and similar strings as false, leaving write tools exposed unlike 1/true. This is a reporter claim without local reproduction or a verified released fix. #3748 reports short-lived tokenized private-repository download URLs in directory results; its author frames it as low-severity hardening, not an established vulnerability, with legitimate large-file download needs. Similar LOCKDOWN claims remain unverified. Classify as configuration/context-exposure experiment candidates. Discussion: fail startup on invalid safety settings and minimize model-visible URLs.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/github/github-mcp-server/issues/3747" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a> · <a href="https://github.com/github/github-mcp-server/issues/3748" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">2</a></p><h3 id="o26-swe-bench-proposes-refusing-unevaluable-empty-results" tabindex="-1">O26 SWE-bench proposes refusing unevaluable empty results <a class="header-anchor" href="#o26-swe-bench-proposes-refusing-unevaluable-empty-results" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O26 SWE-bench proposes refusing unevaluable empty results&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>OPEN PR · 2026-11-08 UTC</strong></p><p>#718 proposes refusing grading when a suite exits nonzero with an empty recorded-failure map, preventing FAIL_ONLY from treating absent results as success. The PR describes 25 upstream tests; it remains OPEN and was not reproduced locally. This does not invalidate all existing SWE-bench scores. Classify as independent-verification/failure-taxonomy experiments. Digital-employee value: separate absence of recorded failure from evidence of completion, without creating a CodeFlowMu development task.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/SWE-bench/SWE-bench/pull/718" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a></p><h3 id="o27-staffdeck-report-exposes-a-possible-sop-tool-output-mismatch" tabindex="-1">O27 StaffDeck report exposes a possible SOP/tool-output mismatch <a class="header-anchor" href="#o27-staffdeck-report-exposes-a-possible-sop-tool-output-mismatch" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O27 StaffDeck report exposes a possible SOP/tool-output mismatch&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>OPEN ISSUE · 2026-11-07 UTC</strong></p><p>#308 reports an SOP requiring per-item comments and URLs while its bound tool returns task-level summary fields; capability_refs filtering can leave textual instructions inconsistent with available actions. The reporter describes results after changing bindings/action nodes; these are not local experiments or proof of an official static contract validator. Competitor learning high, risk pending reproduction; add SOP executability scrutiny without downgrading the whole product. Classify as an experiment candidate: check input/output/action/acceptance-field consistency when saving SOPs.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/OpenBMB/StaffDeck/issues/308" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a></p><h3 id="o28-mcp-apps-report-separates-acknowledgement-from-model-delivery" tabindex="-1">O28 MCP Apps report separates acknowledgement from model delivery <a class="header-anchor" href="#o28-mcp-apps-report-separates-acknowledgement-from-model-delivery" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O28 MCP Apps report separates acknowledgement from model delivery&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>OPEN ISSUE · 2026-11-07 UTC</strong></p><p>ext-apps #852 reports advertised ui/update-model-context capability and successful acknowledgements without an observable indication that updates reach the model, with reporter-observed Web versus desktop/Android differences. SHOULD/MAY language and deferred behavior need separating. A fault across all hosts and a released fix are not established. Classify as protocol-observability tracking and cross-host experience candidates: distinguish receipt, queuing, model consumption and user-visible delivery instead of equating ACK with delivery.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/ext-apps/issues/852" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a></p><h3 id="o29-cursor-adds-voice-access-alongside-background-agent-work" tabindex="-1">O29 Cursor adds voice access alongside background agent work <a class="header-anchor" href="#o29-cursor-adds-voice-access-alongside-background-agent-work" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O29 Cursor adds voice access alongside background agent work&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>OFFICIAL PRODUCT CHANGELOG · 2026-11-06</strong></p><p>The official Voice Chat with Agents update describes talking to a voice agent while coding agents work in the background, across IDE/window/Web/iOS entry points, with short progress updates and full text serving different needs. This verifies the official product description, not tested cross-device state, approval or interruption consistency. Research value is an accessible supervisory interface for digital employees rather than merely shrinking desktop UI. Competitor learning/risk medium-high; retain product-layer observation, not a same-layer FCoP comparison.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://cursor.com/changelog/voice-chat" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a></p><h3 id="o30-deep-agents-async-supervisor-worker-threads-are-a-priority-experience-candidate" tabindex="-1">O30 Deep Agents async supervisor/worker threads are a priority experience candidate <a class="header-anchor" href="#o30-deep-agents-async-supervisor-worker-threads-are-a-priority-experience-candidate" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O30 Deep Agents async supervisor/worker threads are a priority experience candidate&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>OFFICIAL ARTICLE + REFERENCE SOURCE · 2026-11-05</strong></p><p>The official article describes a responsive supervisor and background workers on independent threads, with start/check/update/cancel/list operations, interrupt-or-enqueue steering and approval-aware notification. The async-deep-agents reference uses LangGraph thread/run APIs, a persistent job registry and ASGI/HTTP: Runtime coordination, not a proven same-layer file protocol competitor to FCoP. Repository tool names differ from newer docs, notification wiring requires enabling, and notifications remain beta. Requirements include Python 3.11+/uv or Node 20+/pnpm, model credentials and worker slots. README clone naming is stale and no explicit LICENSE was found; verify before adoption. Not installed. High learning value and a priority experience candidate. Discussion: durable jobs after compaction and reliable notifications during approval.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://www.langchain.com/blog/agents-are-going-async" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a> · <a href="https://github.com/langchain-ai/async-deep-agents/blob/main/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">2</a></p><h3 id="o31-deep-agents-releases-explicit-compaction-refusal-and-checkpoint-evidence" tabindex="-1">O31 Deep Agents releases explicit compaction/refusal and checkpoint evidence <a class="header-anchor" href="#o31-deep-agents-releases-explicit-compaction-refusal-and-checkpoint-evidence" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O31 Deep Agents releases explicit compaction/refusal and checkpoint evidence&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>RELEASED · 2026-11-05 UTC</strong></p><p>deepagents==0.7.33 raises model refusals by default, reports child refusals to parents, records task-call IDs on child checkpoints and adds async notification metadata. Failed compaction receives an explicit error status. 0.7.34 anchors summarization cutoffs by message ID. These are released failure-propagation/causal-link mechanisms, not proof of universally reliable async notification or recovery, nor repair of Codex&#39;s remote compaction service. Classify as failure-visibility/task-evidence experiments; no local runtime results.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://github.com/langchain-ai/deepagents/releases/tag/deepagents%3D%3D0.7.33" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a> · <a href="https://github.com/langchain-ai/deepagents/releases/tag/deepagents%3D%3D0.7.34" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">2</a></p><h3 id="o32-cursor-connects-code-generation-to-shareable-apps-and-publication-gates" tabindex="-1">O32 Cursor connects code generation to shareable apps and publication gates <a class="header-anchor" href="#o32-cursor-connects-code-generation-to-shareable-apps-and-publication-gates" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;O32 Cursor connects code generation to shareable apps and publication gates&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>OFFICIAL PRODUCT CHANGELOG · 2026-11-05</strong></p><p>The Build Live Apps announcement makes app creation, Postgres, Cursor SSO authentication and hosting available on paid plans with cloud agents enabled, using Cursor Origin/GitHub repositories. Publication requires deterministic/agentic security checks and explicit user publication. Team admins can add rules, approve blocked publishes and unpublish apps. This verifies official claims, not tested check coverage, override auditing or rollback. The source provides a date without exact time: this is previously unrecorded material first checked after the same-day baseline, not proof it was published after the previous run finished. Competitor learning/risk high/high; raise application-delivery attention. Study evidence from build through acceptance to authorized publication, including administrator exceptions.</p><p>Original sources：<a href="https://cursor.com/changelog/build-live-apps" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1</a></p><h2 id="layer-2-research-analysis" tabindex="-1">Layer 2: research analysis <a class="header-anchor" href="#layer-2-research-analysis" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Layer 2: research analysis&quot;">​</a></h2><h3 id="a1-responsive-supervision-and-continuous-background-work-now-have-comparable-implementations" tabindex="-1">A1 Responsive supervision and continuous background work now have comparable implementations <a class="header-anchor" href="#a1-responsive-supervision-and-continuous-background-work-now-have-comparable-implementations" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;A1 Responsive supervision and continuous background work now have comparable implementations&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Evidence：O4、O6、O20、O29、O30、O31</p><p>Supported: Deep Agents separates task threads, durable job registries, steering/cancellation and approval-aware notifications; Cursor, Claude and Orca add distinct conversation, background-tool and mobile-control surfaces. They operate at different layers and do not establish a universal digital-employee protocol. Hypothesis: a responsive supervisor plus independent task identity and completion wake-up may inform CodeFlowMu after comparison with its Runtime. Prioritize async-deep-agents experience, checking notification wiring, version drift, licensing, worker capacity and cleanup, then Orca weak-network control. Costs include workers/threads, ordering, deduplication, post-cancel effects and retention. Map chiefly to Runtime/product. Discussion: authoritative jobs after compaction and queued results during approval.</p><h3 id="a2-authority-must-survive-resumption-host-transitions-and-credential-changes" tabindex="-1">A2 Authority must survive resumption, host transitions and credential changes <a class="header-anchor" href="#a2-authority-must-survive-resumption-host-transitions-and-credential-changes" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;A2 Authority must survive resumption, host transitions and credential changes&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Evidence：O2、O3、O5、O6、O8、O18、O19、O22、O25</p><p>Supported: Codex separates project trust/write permission and orders grant persistence; ADK exposes representation-sensitive approval; Paradev opens an explicit owner-cloud-to-host channel while Palmier removes approval friction. These opposing directions cannot all be called governance maturity. Hypothesis: checking subject, scope and revocation throughout delegation/resumption/cross-host calls matters for CodeFlowMu Runtime/adapters and enterprise policy, not automatically FCoP fields. Experiment candidates: resumed permission modes, post-revocation host calls, invalid read-only settings and Enum approvals. Benefits concern explicit authority; costs include policy versions, credentials, recovery compatibility and human blocking. Increase Paradev/Palmier scrutiny; assess Paperclip delivery maturity separately from governance fit.</p><h3 id="a3-recovery-and-completion-require-terminal-evidence-silence-is-not-success" tabindex="-1">A3 Recovery and completion require terminal evidence; silence is not success <a class="header-anchor" href="#a3-recovery-and-completion-require-terminal-evidence-silence-is-not-success" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;A3 Recovery and completion require terminal evidence; silence is not success&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Evidence：O8、O9、O10、O13、O14、O17、O21、O23、O26、O28、O31</p><p>Supported: strict_resume, stale-owner settlement guards, held-call waits, final JSON-RPC flushing, nonzero exits, refusal to grade empty results, compaction error status and ACK/consumption distinctions all clarify state boundaries. Similar fixes support bounded cross-project observations, not comprehensive exactly-once claims. Hypothesis: execution, acceptance, user reporting and website publication should expose separate success/failure/unknown outcomes; local applicability still needs experiments. Candidates: final-record loss, empty tests, restart during approval and duplicate input. Benefits are recovery/accountability; costs include durable state, causal IDs, idempotency and compensation. Compare delivery evidence with FCoP contracts while leaving locks/scheduling/cleanup in Runtime.</p><h3 id="a4-governance-evidence-expands-to-role-declarations-skill-versions-and-independent-signing" tabindex="-1">A4 Governance evidence expands to role declarations, skill versions and independent signing <a class="header-anchor" href="#a4-governance-evidence-expands-to-role-declarations-skill-versions-and-independent-signing" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;A4 Governance evidence expands to role declarations, skill versions and independent signing&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Evidence：O1、O7、O9、O16、O24、O27</p><p>Supported: ARC/Ponder/Tracekit catalogue entries address pre-execution declarations, review processes and external signing; Paperclip pins run-specific skills, Langfuse adds bounded read/download auditing, and StaffDeck&#39;s report suggests SOP/tool-output mismatch. Hypothesis: trace roles through SOPs, capabilities, artifacts and independent acceptance rather than counting logs. Review ARC/Ponder/Tracekit source first, checking original licences, maintenance and trust assumptions before experience; catalogue licence summaries are not adoption reviews. Candidate counterexamples: consistent declarations with runtime overreach, intact signatures with bypassed execution, and acceptable output with missing process evidence. Costs include schema maintenance, signer isolation and privacy-aware retention.</p><h3 id="a5-compare-protocols-and-open-applications-by-semantic-layer-and-operational-limits" tabindex="-1">A5 Compare protocols and open applications by semantic layer and operational limits <a class="header-anchor" href="#a5-compare-protocols-and-open-applications-by-semantic-layer-and-operational-limits" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;A5 Compare protocols and open applications by semantic layer and operational limits&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Evidence：O3、O7、O10、O11、O12、O15、O18、O20、O22、O28、O30、O32</p><p>Supported: A2A adapters retain application authorization/storage duties; A2UI defines UI wire formats; MCP schema/Apps concern tool/context contracts; Deep Agents is thread-based Runtime coordination. This run found insufficient evidence for a new same-layer file-protocol competitor to FCoP. Complementary mechanisms still deserve research. Hypothesis: compare state/events/evidence, routing, negotiation and real adoption before mapping to FCoP, adapters or product. Purpose-driven experience candidates: Deep Agents for async supervision, Paperclip for employee upgrades/skill versions, Orca for mobile control. Check platforms, credentials/services, licensing and migration first. Public source does not establish local operability, nor releases enterprise validation. Local experiments, articles, development reviews and actual comments are all zero. Cursor connects building, security checks and explicit publication, but administrator overrides need separate accountability evidence; map this to product-delivery governance rather than protocol negotiation.</p><h2 id="cross-project-patterns-and-classification" tabindex="-1">Cross-project patterns and classification <a class="header-anchor" href="#cross-project-patterns-and-classification" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Cross-project patterns and classification&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Patterns concern async supervision, continuous authority, explicit failure/unknown states, role/tool contracts and release-versus-operability boundaries. The five analyses synthesize observations and are not additional events. Priority experience candidates are Deep Agents, Paperclip and Orca; other items retain their tracking/reference/experiment classifications. <strong>Local completed experiments 0, articles 0, formal development reviews 0, actual comments 0.</strong> No development tasks were generated; discussion topics are research questions, not submitted comments.</p><h2 id="coverage-dual-source-checks-and-verification-limits" tabindex="-1">Coverage, dual-source checks and verification limits <a class="header-anchor" href="#coverage-dual-source-checks-and-verification-limits" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Coverage, dual-source checks and verification limits&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>Registry: fetched from latest main through connected GitHub tooling; blob SHA <code>8d97df65269aa2b4accd1366d1b71319f99c6efd</code>, 47 researchers. Explicit supplemental projects/competitors remain, giving 88 repository targets. Deep Agents/async-deep-agents are additional local candidates, not Registry edits.</li><li>GitHub: commits, recent issues/PRs and releases were attempted for all 88 targets. Read 1,862 commit records, 219 issue/PR records and 192 release records, including historical material used only for filtering/deduplication. 86 commit windows completed. Orca remains incomplete after four pages/400 records; TSA AI Workforce was inaccessible for all three queries. Their complete baselines are retained. At most five recent issues/PRs and three releases per repository are bounded samples, not historical exhaustive coverage. Selected PR bodies/status/merge times were inspected separately.</li><li>Public social/author pages/official blogs are a separate source type: Cursor&#39;s official changelog, LangChain&#39;s official article and related official documentation were checked; author GitHub/paper entrances support identity/attribution. Two GitHub links are not called dual-source evidence. All 47 authors&#39; personal X replies/threads and other blogs were not exhaustively checked. GitHub primary events can independently support engineering facts.</li><li>The six governance researchers and Yue Zhao&#39;s specified repositories remain queried; omission from observations does not establish no change across all their sources. People/projects are many-to-many; bulbasaur-skill-cli is not labelled AGENTSAFE&#39;s official artifact. Verified Superset→Paradev redirection retains radar provenance.</li><li>Uncovered pre-November-5 history and unfinished pagination remain gaps. Failed triggers and unchecked sources are not quiet success. Events, status, SHAs, windows and classification are recorded locally.</li><li>Publication scope is only today&#39;s bilingual reports and two indexes, using existing site implementation/public snapshots. Source commits, Pages deployment and live click verification are recorded in separate local receipts; this report does not pre-claim deployment success.</li></ul>',153)])])}const m=a(s,[["render",n]]);export{h as __pageData,m as default};
